From: "Barak A. Pearlmutter" <bap@debian.org>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 10:23:22 +0100
Subject: make installed cppad/configure.hpp architecture independent

Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1133789
Forwarded: https://github.com/coin-or/CppAD/issues/258

libcppad-dev is Multi-Arch: same, so every file it ships below /usr/include
must be identical on all architectures.  Three of the values that the cmake
command substitutes into cppad/configure.hpp depend on the size of size_t
and of a pointer, so the installed header differed between 32-bit and 64-bit
architectures and the package was not co-installable.

Determine those three symbols with the preprocessor instead, using the
compiler-supplied __SIZEOF_* macros; this reproduces exactly the values that
the cmake tests produced on each architecture.  A compiler that does not
define those macros would silently evaluate them as zero and so get the
wrong answer, hence the #error guards.

The tape address type test assumes CPPAD_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE is unsigned int,
which is the cmake default and what Debian builds with.
---
 include/cppad/configure.hpp.in | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/cppad/configure.hpp.in b/include/cppad/configure.hpp.in
index 9eb936d..7667aab 100644
--- a/include/cppad/configure.hpp.in
+++ b/include/cppad/configure.hpp.in
@@ -240,8 +240,13 @@ CPPAD_IS_SAME_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE_SIZE_T
 Is size_t the type the same as CPPAD_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE.
 {xrst_spell_off}
 {xrst_code hpp} */
-# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE_SIZE_T \
-   @cppad_is_same_tape_addr_type_size_t@
+# if !defined(__SIZEOF_SIZE_T__) || !defined(__SIZEOF_INT__)
+# error "cppad: compiler does not define __SIZEOF_SIZE_T__ and __SIZEOF_INT__"
+# elif __SIZEOF_SIZE_T__ == __SIZEOF_INT__
+# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE_SIZE_T 1
+# else
+# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_TAPE_ADDR_TYPE_SIZE_T 0
+# endif
 /* {xrst_code}
 {xrst_spell_off}
 
@@ -348,7 +353,13 @@ CPPAD_IS_SAME_UNSIGNED_INT_SIZE_T
 *********************************
 If true, ``unsigned int`` and ``size_t`` are the same type
 {xrst_code hpp} */
-# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_UNSIGNED_INT_SIZE_T @cppad_is_same_unsigned_int_size_t@
+# if !defined(__SIZEOF_SIZE_T__) || !defined(__SIZEOF_INT__)
+# error "cppad: compiler does not define __SIZEOF_SIZE_T__ and __SIZEOF_INT__"
+# elif __SIZEOF_SIZE_T__ == __SIZEOF_INT__
+# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_UNSIGNED_INT_SIZE_T 1
+# else
+# define CPPAD_IS_SAME_UNSIGNED_INT_SIZE_T 0
+# endif
 /* {xrst_code}
 
 CPPAD_PADDING_BLOCK_T
@@ -356,7 +367,14 @@ CPPAD_PADDING_BLOCK_T
 Is a string used to define an object that pads the block_t structure
 so that its size is a multiple of the size of a double.
 {xrst_code hpp} */
-# define CPPAD_PADDING_BLOCK_T @cppad_padding_block_t@
+# if !defined(__SIZEOF_SIZE_T__) || !defined(__SIZEOF_POINTER__) \
+   || !defined(__SIZEOF_DOUBLE__)
+# error "cppad: compiler does not define the __SIZEOF_* macros"
+# elif (2 * __SIZEOF_SIZE_T__ + __SIZEOF_POINTER__) % __SIZEOF_DOUBLE__
+# define CPPAD_PADDING_BLOCK_T void* note_used_;
+# else
+# define CPPAD_PADDING_BLOCK_T
+# endif
 /* {xrst_code}
 
 {xrst_end configure.hpp}
